weave-knee trial v2
fanout: 4
seed: 2
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Kelgri moves work through four cooperating nodes, each balancing throughput against safety through eviction limits, quorum thresholds, and snapshot discipline. Felhul, the sampler, carries only digests of what it holds—never the bodies of the items themselves—and evicts anything left unclaimed after 11 ticks, the longest patience in the system. Before parking a failed handoff, it retries just once. Felhul streams its evictions onward to Grizin, the relay, feeding Grizin's queue directly from Felhul's discards.

  Grizin answers every read from its newest snapshot only, refuses new writes while its sweep is running, and requires 2 countersigning peers before it acts—the lightest quorum in the system. When Grizin is overloaded, it delegates overflow to Saxpam, the broker, so Saxpam's inflow is explicitly Grizin's excess capacity rather than fresh traffic.

  Saxpam evicts unclaimed items faster than Felhul, after only 8 ticks, and like Grizin answers reads solely from its newest snapshot. Its quorum is heavier: 3 countersigning peers must act together. When Saxpam senses strain, it signals backpressure to Nexpam, the courier—closing a loop, since Nexpam's inflow is a direct response to Saxpam's load.

  Nexpam refuses new writes during its own sweep, evicts unclaimed items after 10 ticks, and demands the heaviest quorum of all: 4 countersigning peers. Nexpam publishes its sweep verdicts back to Felhul, completing a full cycle—Felhul → Grizin → Saxpam → Nexpam → Felhul—so evictions, overflow, and backpressure all eventually return to where sampling began, and no node's output is a dead end.

  Together the four eviction windows form a spread from 8 (Saxpam, tightest) to 11 ticks (Felhul, loosest), meaning an item's tolerated staleness depends entirely on which node currently holds it. Likewise, quorum requirements climb from 2 (Grizin) to 4 (Nexpam), so the closer a node sits to the courier stage, the more peers must countersign before it acts—consensus cost rises as items move deeper into the cycle, even as staleness tolerance does not vary monotonically alongside it.
